i love a good rainfall  the sound the smell and the mist on my  skin  it's all very relaxing  but i don't think i'd be able to enjoy  it like this if i didn't have a safe dry  place to enjoy it from  i love the feeling of coldness and the  wind on my skin because i know there's  warmth and a shelter place in just a  couple footsteps a really great home a  house that doesn't just provide shelter  but a house that inspires you and  invigorates your senses  a house like that it lets us experience  both protection and exposure  by carefully mediating between the  outside and the inside world  hey guys welcome back to the channel if  you're new here my name is dammy and i  am a licensed architect in a very  wet rainy vancouver vc  on this channel we talk about  architecture and design and today we'll  be talking about design strategies that  take advantage of the condition  environments  and also the exposed unconditioned  environments this is a part of a design  series where i try to break down some of  the lessons that i learned on my road  trip where i visited 111 frank lloyd  wright buildings scattered across north  america as well as some proven  strategies from books like the good  house and the good old ching book  i'll leave the links to these books  below  so apparently frank lloyd wright  jokingly said a good building should let  the rain lincoln a little because he  understood what a wonderful experience  it is to experience the weather from a  dry safe comforts of your home i'm sure  there's architects who introduce water  in accidentally  but once your building is watertight  introducing water as a experiential  element  it can really elevate your designs  there's a couple ways you can do this  you can introduce a roof with deep eaves  where people can sit inside near an open  window  and  watch the landscape and listen to the  rain and smell the rain in this case the  overhang forms a boundary between the  wet and the dry areas  a porch is also a wonderful thing  it can serve as an extension of the  living room it animates the building  it animates the streetscape and it's a  place where your family can get together  to experience a summer storm you can  also use little details to introduce  water  japanese homes typically have a deep  overhang and the water from the roof  drains down an exposed drain rather than  through a downspout and it creates this  really delicate pattern of light when  the water trickles down the chain  frank lloyd wright was heavily inspired  by japanese architecture and he  incorporated chains into a lot of his  designs one of his homeowners said that  when the rain falls it sounds like a  waterfall in the summer the chain  becomes a memory of the rainfall  i think one of the best things in life  is sitting by a campfire on a chilly  evening you could incorporate this into  your house by introducing an outdoor  fireplace or a fire circle in the  backyard or an outdoor barbecue pit if  you want to incorporate an element you  can touch  materials with thermal mass and a south  facing wall is also an elegant solution  thermal mass of course is the ability  for the material to absorb and hold heat  for a long period of time which is why  when you sit on a stone bench on a sunny  day you can feel the heat on your skin  frank lloyd wright used not just  thermally massive materials but radiant  floor heating in all of his homes in his  signature cherokee red floor tiles he  was the first architect in north america  to popularize the system and fun fact  a lot of people think he took this when  he was on his trip to japan which is  true  but  radiant floors were never a part of  traditional japanese architecture  when he was in japan he was invited to a  japanese home  and the homeowners had visited korea  which actually does have a very long  history of using heated stones to warm  the floors and it's called under and i  actually have very distinct memories of  sitting and playing and sleeping on  the warm floors of my grandma's house  when i was a kid and this is definitely  something i want to incorporate into my  own home someday  you could also include a south-facing  reading nook where you can sit and take  in the warmth of the sun even when the  room itself is cool  another way of linking hot and cold is  to create an outdoor room that's open to  the garden or the yard and it's  partially roofed and it's surrounded by  at least two to three walls of the house  to create a sense of enclosure the room  is typically large enough to have a  dining table and chairs  and  it has a fireplace to warm you in the  evenings during the hot summers it  creates a border between the cool  interior and the hot exterior an nook is  a very nice way to connect the fire and  the rest of the room typically it has a  lower ceiling than the rest of the room  and benches at right angles on either  sides and because it's an enclosed  environment that's still a part of the  rest of the room you can experience both  the controlled warmth of the fireplace  and the coolness of the rest of the room  so my friends and i go camping in bc  once a year and we've been doing this  for about 10 years now and one of the  years we decided  okay let's just invest in a good tent  because there's five of us and we always  bring our own separate tents and we  usually don't have space for it in the  car  and it's just a huge hassle and so we  were just like okay let's go to costco  let's invest in a good tent and so this  tent is amazing  it fits all five of us and there's a top  layer that can be opened up and  it just leaves this  mesh and sometimes we  just open it up when it's warm so that  we can look at the stars  and it just brings in this  wonderful  refreshing  warm summer breeze that is just so  delightful and  although you're sleeping  on the ground  on an air mattress  i  get the most amazing night sleep  i think the feeling of wind on your skin  is a tactile and it's a very pleasant  experience  but if you don't properly protect the  space by contrasting it with still air  it can get pretty  unpleasant  a partly enclosed courtyard is a good  way to control the wind so that you can  experience a little bit of both when we  went to visit the alhambra in granada we  had to walk up this hill and our whole  walk up there was very very windy  and as soon as we walked into the  courtyard i was amazed by  the stillness  and quiet and you know i couldn't hear  the wind and i think more than the  visual  separation the acoustic and tactile  separation made the space feel much more  unique there are some ways to replicate  this condition when you're designing a  house in the 1920s sleeping porches were  very popular  where you had a bed in a cover porch or  an outdoor living room and that just  sounds so wonderful  it kind of reminds me of being a kid  again and finally i'd like to share a  quick message from our sponsor pacific  arc who have kindly provided me with all  the tools that i'm using in this video  so when i was in architecture school i  couldn't afford to buy nice pens or  pencils because they're all quite  expensive pacific art is great because  they carry high quality architecture  supplies at a very affordable price i  think good quality tools should be  available to everyone no matter where  you are in your architectural journey  because  it's a very long journey  and the tools that you draw with it  makes a really big difference  if you enjoy sketching with something  the chances are you are going to sketch  more which is how you get better i've  been working with pacific arc to curate  a kit of products on their website which  you can find in the description  and if you use my promo code you can get  an additional 20 off all of their  products if you're interested in more  design strategies check out my other  video where i show you how you can  control the inside and outside  conditions to create contrast when  you're designing a house 